#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106659} :activity/id #uuid "ca486896-39b1-5972-b175-42e6a76d9594" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049613 :topic/id #uuid "049a25fe-c3b5-5ff3-aebd-919c2185e3f2" :topic/math-academy-id 1270} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106659 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186107086 :tutorial/content "When a circle is not centered at the origin, we can still write its equation in polar form. In fact, the polar equation of a circle is particularly straightforward when it passes through the origin and its center lies on the $x$-axis.\n\nFor example, let $a$ be some nonzero real number, and consider a circle of radius $\\mid a \\mid$ with center at the point $(a,0)$.\n\n![](images/46/4632ae0baaced6ba3699fddf859b71226b5fcebe4700bbbdf4774d1d701e53ad.png)\n\nWith a little bit of geometry and trigonometry, it turns out that the equation of this circle in polar form is given by\n\n$$\nr=2a\\cos\\theta\n$$\n\nWe'll derive this result at the end of the lesson.\n\nSo, for instance, if $a=2$, then we have a circle of radius $2$ centered at $(2,0)$, and its polar equation is\n\n$$\nr= 4 \\cos\\theta\n$$\n\nSimilarly, if $a=-2$, then we have a circle of radius\n\n$$\n\\mid {-2} \\mid = 2\n$$\n\ncentered at $(-2,0)$, and its polar equation is\n\n$$\nr= -4 \\cos\\theta\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "0188970c-f17c-55e7-814d-257c054f1f3b" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3509 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "c75aacbb-02ff-51ce-ba99-d3b55a1383cf" :step/math-academy-id 19877 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106792}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106660 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105758 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105610 :question/id #uuid "fb897f41-dd8e-561f-be0e-02d2f9972203" :question/math-academy-id "e-5393"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "92b54d77-fce2-53bf-aaad-4c7db8285162" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106071} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106072}] :knowledge-point/title "The Polar Equation of a Circle Centered on the Y-Axis"} :step/id #uuid "8a41da03-9799-5f4c-8366-eca36182172d" :step/math-academy-id 19879 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106662}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106661 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186107087 :tutorial/content "Now, let $a$ be some nonzero real number, and let's consider a circle of radius $\\mid a \\mid$ whose center is at the point $(0,a)$.\n\n![](images/40/40477171cd880e675656ed0093c26a85ebe02b7939ec9421a1bcc52c763a68fe.png)\n\nNotice that this circle passes through the origin, and its center lies on the $y$-axis.\n\nAgain, using some geometry and trigonometry, it's possible to show that the polar equation of the circle is\n\n$$\nr=2a \\sin \\theta\n$$\n\nWe'll derive this result at the end of the lesson.\n\nSo, for instance, if $a=2$, then we have a circle of radius $2$ centered at $(0,2)$, and its polar equation is\n\n$$\nr=4 \\sin \\theta\n$$\n\nSimilarly, if $a=-3$, then we have a circle of radius\n\n$$\n\\mid {-3} \\mid = 3\n$$\n\ncentered at $(0,-3)$, and its polar equation is\n\n$$\nr=-6 \\sin \\theta\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "ce8a6f30-10e5-5d3d-8331-f7a9bd767204" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3510 :tutorial/title "The Polar Equation of a Circle Centered on the Y-Axis"} :step/id #uuid "55bc57c5-e879-57a9-8620-d5ced457c8ee" :step/math-academy-id 19880 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106660}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106662 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105759 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105611 :question/id #uuid "9958848d-a3ec-599e-bbc4-44b3993bc707" :question/math-academy-id "e-5394"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "22b3da96-2573-513e-ae70-2b49510acd87" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106079} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106080}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Polar Equation of a Circle Given Its Graph"} :step/id #uuid "39d55595-51da-541e-8bd9-405d1dcce755" :step/math-academy-id 19881 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106664}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106664 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105761 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105613 :question/id #uuid "7e0646a3-f4d2-5a29-ac05-519494a06177" :question/math-academy-id "e-5396"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1ad4cf0e-4346-5277-9026-122b5ae58f25" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048292}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106074} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106081}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Polar Equation of a Circle Given a Point on the Circumference"} :step/id #uuid "29ea04d0-1517-5ed0-ab95-21b1ab2e1718" :step/math-academy-id 19883 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106923}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106792 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105836 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105669 :question/id #uuid "dd2bf9b1-9198-5040-9ef4-9924b4181108" :question/math-academy-id "e-7598"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e3504bc1-05d2-55c5-b862-84be2e2516f7" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050087}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106070} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106073}] :knowledge-point/title "The Polar Equation of a Circle Centered on the X-Axis"} :step/id #uuid "08506d5f-4ac6-5cb7-be27-185b7dd2b066" :step/math-academy-id 28345 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106661}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106923 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186107028 :tutorial/content "![](images/46/4632ae0baaced6ba3699fddf859b71226b5fcebe4700bbbdf4774d1d701e53ad.png)\n\nSuppose we want to derive the polar equation of a circle with\n\n- center at $(a, 0)$, where $a \\ne 0$, and\n- radius $\\mid a \\mid$, so that it passes through the origin.\n\nLet a point $P$ on the circle have polar coordinates $(r, \\theta)$. Then, the Cartesian coordinates of $P$ are\n\n$$\n(x, y) = (r\\cos\\theta, \\, r\\sin\\theta)\n$$\n\nThe distance from $P$ to the center $(a, 0)$ is equal to the radius $\\mid a \\mid$. We can describe this using the distance formula:\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{(r\\cos\\theta - a)^2 + (r\\sin\\theta)^2} = \\mid a \\mid\n$$\n\nSquaring both sides, we get\n\n$$\n(r\\cos\\theta - a)^2 + (r\\sin\\theta)^2 = a^2\n$$\n\nExpanding both terms yields\n\n$$\nr^2\\cos^2\\theta - 2ar\\cos\\theta + a^2 + r^2\\sin^2\\theta = a^2\n$$\n\nThen, we combine like terms:\n\n$$\nr^2(\\cos^2\\theta + \\sin^2\\theta) - 2ar\\cos\\theta + a^2 = a^2\n$$\n\nSince\n\n$$\n\\cos^2\\theta + \\sin^2\\theta = 1\n$$\n\n, we can simplify the above as follows:\n\n$$\nr^2 - 2ar\\cos\\theta + a^2 = a^2\n$$\n\nSubtracting $a^2$ from both sides gives\n\n$$\nr^2 - 2ar\\cos\\theta = 0\n$$\n\nThen, we factor our equation:\n\n$$\nr(r - 2a\\cos\\theta) = 0\n$$\n\nSo, either $r = 0$ (which corresponds to the origin, already on the circle), or\n\n$$\nr = 2a\\cos\\theta\n$$\n\nwhich is the desired polar equation of the circle." :tutorial/id #uuid "f0eb1b8d-075c-5b34-a2c1-5cb900398a10" :tutorial/math-academy-id 13319 :tutorial/title "Deriving the Polar Equation of a Circle Centered at (a,0)"} :step/id #uuid "8a681a0f-8eba-55a1-b05e-b4aa4557b465" :step/math-academy-id 464110 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106924}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106924 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186107029 :tutorial/content "![](images/a2/a2662be5d74155ca29b600cf48982a1ee2b0db62459334af7784394cabea8687.png)\n\nSuppose we want to derive the polar equation of a circle with\n\n- center at $(0, a)$, where $a \\ne 0$, and\n- radius $\\mid a \\mid$, so that it passes through the origin.\n\nLet a point $P$ on the circle have polar coordinates $(r, \\theta)$. Then, the Cartesian coordinates of $P$ are\n\n$$\n(x, y) = (r\\cos\\theta, \\, r\\sin\\theta)\n$$\n\nThe distance from $P$ to the center $(0, a)$ is equal to the radius $\\mid a \\mid$. We can describe this using the distance formula:\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{(r\\cos\\theta - 0)^2 + (r\\sin\\theta - a)^2} = \\mid a \\mid\n$$\n\nSquaring both sides, we get\n\n$$\n(r\\cos\\theta)^2 + (r\\sin\\theta - a)^2 = a^2\n$$\n\nExpanding both terms yields\n\n$$\nr^2\\cos^2\\theta + r^2\\sin^2\\theta - 2ar\\sin\\theta + a^2 = a^2\n$$\n\nThen, we combine like terms:\n\n$$\nr^2(\\cos^2\\theta + \\sin^2\\theta) - 2ar\\sin\\theta + a^2 = a^2\n$$\n\nSince\n\n$$\n\\cos^2\\theta + \\sin^2\\theta = 1\n$$\n\n, we simplify:\n\n$$\nr^2 - 2ar\\sin\\theta + a^2 = a^2\n$$\n\nSubtracting $a^2$ from both sides gives\n\n$$\nr^2 - 2ar\\sin\\theta = 0\n$$\n\nThen, we factor our equation:\n\n$$\nr(r - 2a\\sin\\theta) = 0\n$$\n\nSo, either $r = 0$ (which corresponds to the origin, already on the circle), or\n\n$$\nr = 2a\\sin\\theta\n$$\n\nwhich is the desired polar equation of the circle." :tutorial/id #uuid "8215bce2-13e6-53d9-a691-505556bd572d" :tutorial/math-academy-id 13320 :tutorial/title "Deriving the Polar Equation of a Circle Centered at (0,a)"} :step/id #uuid "e723ee8a-09d9-58ba-ae10-4ce50061926a" :step/math-academy-id 464112}] :activity/title "Polar Equations of Circles Centered on the Coordinate Axes" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186105952}]}
